Finding, 2026-09-19 — elicitation answer set #1 (current, live on the artifact)
================================================================================

When answering the 194-question elicitation questionnaire (Phase E4), the discrimination
order that emerged, wherever a homophone group contained more than one grammatical
person, was:

    pers_1 (I)  >  pers_2 (you)  >  pers_3 (he/she/it)

i.e. wherever pers_1 was present in a group, it was almost always left unmarked (∅, the
bare/canonical form), and pers_2/pers_3 carried whatever marker was needed to tell them
apart from it and from each other.

Measured consequence (from elicitation_answers.json, 194 answered oppositions):
- Every opposition where a pers_3 (singular, nbr_s) reading appears on one side and
  pers_1 unmarked on the other: pers_1 stayed empty, pers_3 got marked -- 14 such
  pairs.
- Across every opposition where a pers_3 (nbr_s) reading appears at all (either side),
  it needed an explicit marker in 58 of 59 cases. Only ONE opposition left pers_3 (nbr_s)
  unmarked: q1, avoir's "a" (ind présent 3s) vs "as" (ind présent 2s) -- here pers_2
  ("as") carried the marker instead, so "a" got to be the ∅ default of that particular
  pair (no pers_1 competitor was present in that group).

Second-order consequence: because pers_3 (singular) so often needed its own marker just
to be distinguished from pers_1/pers_2, that same marker also had to be RE-asserted on
the pers_3-plural reading (pers_3:nbr_p) in every 3-way cluster that also contained a
plural form -- i.e. discriminating bare "pers_3" from "pers_3:nbr_p" became its own
recurring sub-problem, redundantly re-marking pers_3 on both the singular and the plural
side. 9 such oppositions were found:

  q7   être    était (pers_3) vs étaient (pers_3, nbr_p)
  q38  être    soit (subjonctif, pers_3) vs soient (nbr_p, pers_3, subjonctif)
  q39  pouvoir pourrait (pers_3) vs pourraient (nbr_p, pers_3)
  q50  voir    voit (pers_3) vs voient (pers_3, subjonctif, nbr_p)
  q64  croire  croit (pers_3) vs croient (nbr_p, pers_3)
  q83  rire    rit (passé, pers_3) vs rient (nbr_p, pers_3)
  q104 enfuir  enfuie (subjonctif, pers_3) vs enfuient (pers_3, nbr_p)
  q130 mourir  mourrait (conditionnel, pers_3) vs mouraient (pers_3, nbr_p)
  q131 mourir  mourait (pers_3) vs mourraient (conditionnel, nbr_p, pers_3)

This is a VALID way to resolve homophone discrimination order -- there is nothing wrong
with preferring pers_1 as the canonical/silent default; it is a legitimate design choice
about which grammatical person a writer's reflexes treat as "the bare form." It is
recorded here so the choice and its measured effect aren't lost, ahead of trying a second
calibration (see the "pers_3 as default" answer set) for comparison.

Resulting Phase G grouping under this model (after the two q130/q152 corrections):
- 13 live markers, 0 conflicts across all 47,799 homophone groups, K = 7 keypresses.
- Keypress groups (one greedy-coloring run; exact bundling can vary run to run, K does
  not): {imparfait, pers_2}, {conditionnel, future, passé}, {infinitif, pers_3},
  {impératif}, {f, nbr_p}, {p}, {pers_1, subjonctif}.
- Unpressable (never needed a marker): VER, indicatif, m, nbr_s, participe, présent, s.

